      "latency_ms": 22957.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1107,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more electronegative (it pulls electrons more strongly) than hydrogen, the electrons in the bond are pulled toward the oxygen. This creates a \"bent\" shape where one side of the molecule has a slight negative charge and the other side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to interact with other substances.\n\n### 2. The Universal Solvent\nBecause of its polarity, water is excellent at dissolving other substances. It is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is crucial for biological processes, allowing nutrients to move through cells and waste to be removed.\n\n### 3. States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist as a solid, liquid, and gas at the temperatures naturally found on Earth:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure that is less dense than liquid water (which is why ice floats).\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking, washing, and swimming.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor):** When heated, water turns into a gas that rises into the atmosphere.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant compound on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body.\n\n### 5. Importance to Life\nWater is essential for life as we know it. It acts as a medium for chemical reactions, helps regulate body temperature (sweating and panting), transports nutrients and oxygen to cells, and provides the medium in which life began billions of years ago."
